_id":"OBMD011700","measurement_id":"MCTM-039216","value":"14.6 ± 2.0","numeric":14.6}}],"logp":[{"abbreviation_in_the_article":"(22+)","value":-0.2,"value_relation":"=","type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"PBS","pH":7.4,"doi":"10.1002/cmdc.201700240","year":2017,"counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","record_id":"MLDB-000155","detection":"UV-Vis","method_description":"The lipophilicity of the ruthenium(II) complexes was determined using the shake-flask technique37 with 1-octanol and PBS (150 mm sodium chloride, 10 mm sodium phosphate, pH 7.4). PBS buffer is generally considered a more appropriate model of blood serum than pure water, as it better approximates the ionic strength and pH of blood plasma. The solute (33.3×10-5 m) was dissolved first in octanol and PBS, then the two saturated phases were shaken for 30 min at room temperature, and then let to equilibrate for 24 h. After this period, the distribution of the complex in each solvent was measured using a Hewlett–Packard HP8453A spectrophotometer. The corresponding concentration of the solute in each solvent was determined using Beer's law from the absorbance of the measured spectra; the concentration was then used in calculating the partition coefficient. The partition coefficient (Po/w) is the ratio of the equilibrium concentration of the dissolved compound in two phases [Eq. 1]: logPo/w = log([solute]octanol / [solute]water). The logP of the ruthenium complexes was also obtained for a biphasic solution of DI water and 1-octanol using the same general procedure above."},{"abbreviation_in_the_article":"(22+)","value":-1.3,"value_relation":"=","type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"water","doi":"10.1002/cmdc.201700240","year":2017,"counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","record_id":"MLDB-000167","detection":"UV-Vis","method_description":"The lipophilicity of the ruthenium(II) complexes was determined using the shake-flask technique37 with 1-octanol and PBS (150 mm sodium chloride, 10 mm sodium phosphate, pH 7.4). PBS buffer is generally considered a more appropriate model of blood serum than pure water, as it better approximates the ionic strength and pH of blood plasma. The solute (33.3×10-5 m) was dissolved first in octanol and PBS, then the two saturated phases were shaken for 30 min at room temperature, and then let to equilibrate for 24 h. After this period, the distribution of the complex in each solvent was measured using a Hewlett–Packard HP8453A spectrophotometer. The corresponding concentration of the solute in each solvent was determined using Beer's law from the absorbance of the measured spectra; the concentration was then used in calculating the partition coefficient. The partition coefficient (Po/w) is the ratio of the equilibrium concentration of the dissolved compound in two phases [Eq. 1]: logPo/w = log([solute]octanol / [solute]water). The logP of the ruthenium complexes was also obtained for a biphasic solution of DI water and 1-octanol using the same general procedure above."},{"abbreviation_in_the_article":2,"value":-0.76,"value_relation":"=","type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"0.9% NaCl","temperature_C":25,"doi":"10.1021/jm501095r","year":2014,"counterion":"[O-][Cl+3]([O-])([O-])[O-].[O-][Cl+3]([O-])([O-])[O-]","record_id":"MLDB-001534","detection":"ICP-MS","method_description":"Lipophilicity was presented as log Po/w values, which were determined by the flask-shaking method. An aliquot of a stock solution of the ruthenium complex in aqueous NaCl (0.9% w/v and saturated with octanol) was added to an equal volume of octanol (saturated with 0.9% NaCl w/v), and the mixture was shaken overnight at 60 rpm to allow partitioning at 298 K. After the sample was centrifuged at 3000 rpm for 10 min, the aqueous layer was carefully separated from the octanol layer for ruthenium analysis. The Ru concentration in the aqueous phase was determined by ICP-MS and used to calculate the [Ru]O/[Ru]W ratio."},{"abbreviation_in_the_article":"rac-[Ru(phen)2(dppz)]2+","value":-1.47,"value_relation":"=","std":0.02,"type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"water","temperature_C":25,"doi":"10.1021/acs.inorgchem.5c04859","year":2025,"counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","record_id":"MLDB-004553","detection":"UV-Vis","method_description":"The octanol/water partition coefficient, log P measurement was carried out using an adaptation of the shake-flask method based on a literature method1 and guided by OECD test protocol 107.2 Octanol (10 mL) and either HPLC-grade H2O or phosphate buffered saline (PBS) (for Log P; pH 7.4) were mutually saturated by shaking the biphasic mixture of the two phases overnight at ambient temperature. An aqueous stock of complex solution of known concentration was added to 800 μL octanol to calculate the relative response factor of the complex in octanol. Each complex solution was added to 1.00 mL aqueous phase to a final concentration of 20 μM and shaken with 1.00 mL aqueous saturated octanol for 2 h at 25 °C. The neat phases were partitioned and each sample analysed inS3triplicate by UV-vis absorption spectroscopy to determine the concentration in each layer and the LogP calculated according to equation (1). At pH 7.4, Log P = Log D for non-ionisable compounds. Log Po/w = log([Ru]octanol / [Ru]water)"},{"abbreviation_in_the_article":"rac-[Ru(phen)2(dppz)]2+","value":-0.32,"value_relation":"=","std":0.01,"type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"PBS","temperature_C":25,"doi":"10.1021/acs.inorgchem.5c04859","year":2025,"counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","record_id":"MLDB-004555","detection":"UV-Vis","method_description":"The octanol/water partition coefficient, log P measurement was carried out using an adaptation of the shake-flask method based on a literature method1 and guided by OECD test protocol 107.2 Octanol (10 mL) and either HPLC-grade H2O or phosphate buffered saline (PBS) (for Log P; pH 7.4) were mutually saturated by shaking the biphasic mixture of the two phases overnight at ambient temperature. An aqueous stock of complex solution of known concentration was added to 800 μL octanol to calculate the relative response factor of the complex in octanol. Each complex solution was added to 1.00 mL aqueous phase to a final concentration of 20 μM and shaken with 1.00 mL aqueous saturated octanol for 2 h at 25 °C. The neat phases were partitioned and each sample analysed inS3triplicate by UV-vis absorption spectroscopy to determine the concentration in each layer and the LogP calculated according to equation (1). At pH 7.4, Log P = Log D for non-ionisable compounds. Log Po/w = log([Ru]octanol / [Ru]water)"},{"abbreviation_in_the_article":"[Ru(phen)2(dppz)]2+","value":-1.45,"value_relation":"=","type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"water","doi":"10.1002/cplu.201800042","year":2018,"counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","record_id":"MLDB-004999","detection":"UV-Vis","method_description":"The measurement of the octanol-water partition coefficients were performed using the shake-flask method. The chloride salt of the ruthenium complex (0.1 mM) was dissolved in the water phase and an equal volume of n-octanol was added. After saturation of both phases by shaking overnight at room temperature, the concentration of the complex in each phase was determined spectroscopically at 450 nm."},{"abbreviation_in_the_article":"Ru3Cl2","value":-2.9,"value_relation":"=","type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"water","temperature_C":25,"doi":"10.1021/acsami.6b07038","year":2016,"counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","record_id":"MLDB-005027","detection":"UV-Vis","method_description":"The partition coefficients (log P) were measured according to the reported procedure with minor modification. Aqueous solution of ruthenium complex with counter ion of Cl or PF6 was prepared at 50 uM. Then an equal volume of n-octanol was added. The two phases were mutually saturated by shaking 12 h at 25 C and allowed to separate by centrifugation. The concentration of the metal complex in each phase was determined on a UV-Vis spectrophotometer at maximum MLCT absorption wavelength."},{"abbreviation_in_the_article":"Ru3(PF6)2","value":-1.39,"value_relation":"=","type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"water","temperature_C":25,"doi":"10.1021/acsami.6b07038","year":2016,"counterion":"F[P-](F)(F)(F)(F)F.F[P-](F)(F)(F)(F)F","record_id":"MLDB-005028","detection":"UV-Vis","method_description":"The partition coefficients (log P) were measured according to the reported procedure with minor modification. Aqueous solution of ruthenium complex with counter ion of Cl or PF6 was prepared at 50 uM. Then an equal volume of n-octanol was added. The two phases were mutually saturated by shaking 12 h at 25 C and allowed to separate by centrifugation. The concentration of the metal complex in each phase was determined on a UV-Vis spectrophotometer at maximum MLCT absorption wavelength."}]}